  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   - Central idea: The current solution achieves C=0.3809, while the target is 0.38085. We will use a combination of multi-resolution optimization and advanced local search methods to escape local minima. The approach involves:\n     - Progressive resolution increase from low to high dimensions\n     - Hybrid optimization combining analytical gradients with stochastic perturbations\n     - Constraint preservation using iterative clipping and rescaling\n   - Rationale:\n     - The correlation objective is highly non-convex with complex topology\n     - Higher resolution allows finer features to reduce overlap\n     - Multi-start optimization with clones and strategic perturbations\n     - Balanced exploitation-exploration using adaptive tempering schedules\n   - Limitations: Requires careful handling of dimensionality growth (up to 2560 points) and must maintain constraint satisfaction without introducing artifacts\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Input: Discrete function samples h on [0,2] with \u2211h*dx=1 and 0\u2264h\u22641\n   - Objective: minimize max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx = (1/n)\u00b7max lag correlation\n   - Constraints: h(x) \u2208 [0,1] and \u222bh(x)dx=1\n   - Representation: Discrete samples h=[h\u2080,h\u2081,...,h_{n-1}] on uniform grid\n   - Optimization: BFGS-UG with analytical projected gradients and stochastic adaptive tempering\n   - Small-scale test: Verify objective calculation with correlation modes and integral preservation\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   a) Initialize at parent resolution (640 points)\n   - Phase 1: Generate three types of initial candidates:\n        Type 1: Clipped parent function\n        Type 2: Random restarts with sum constraint\n        Type 3: Dispersed points with binary mixing\n   - Phase 2: Multi-resolution descent:\n        Double resolution (1280 points) for expansive search\n        Then quadruple (2560) for deepest search\n   b) Iterative local search:\n        Use BFGS with nonlinear constraints (CVXPY)\n        Define gradient projection: For i in [0,n-1]:\n            dh = small perturbation\n            corr_old = current correlation\n            corr_new = new correlation with h[i]\u00b1dh\n            gradient[i] = (corr_new - corr_old)/(2*dh)\n        Project onto [0,1] using minimum-norm projection\n   c) Adaptive tempering:\n        For k=1-10:\n            T_base = [100, 50, 20, 10, 5, 3, 2, 1]  # Tempering schedule\n            T = current temperature * exp(-k)\n            if T<1.0: break\n            Compute gradient with noise magnitude proportional to 1/T\n            Use BFGS with step size adaptive to gradient norm\n   d) Constraint enforcement:\n        Use projected gradients to maintain bounds\n        For sum constraint: Regularized interpolation\n            h_interp = h_prev + \u03bb*(target_sum - current_sum)/n\n            h_interp = clip(h_interp, 0,1)\n   e) Termination:\n        Check convergence with gradient smallness or stagnation\n        Require 10 resolution steps without improvement\n        Save penalization count and recent best values\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   - Perturbation strategies: \n     Type A: Uniform random walk (used for exploration)\n     Type B: Cauchy-distributed jitter (used near optimum)\n     Type C: Inversion around mean (used during tempering steps)\n   - Learning components:\n     Auto-encoder for low-dimensional projections at coarse resolutions\n     Historical best analysis for candidate generation\n     Dynamic selection of perturbation methods based on local gradient\n   - Independent variations to be explored:\n     1) Fractional sampling (using 50% resolution samples)\n     2) Non-uniform grids\n     3) Noise-shaped quantization\n     4) Edge-adaptive periodic boundary removal\n     5) Simulated annealing with adaptive neighborhood\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - Time allocation:\n     Phase 1: 20% of budget (\u2248260 seconds) for resolution search\n     Phase 2: 70% for fine optimization\n     Phase 3: 10% for validation and fallback\n   - Contingency:\n     - Low resolution path if high resolution fails to converge\n     - Dynamic dimension reduction when correlation stabilizes\n     - Fallback candidate from previous resolution on time expiration\n   - Handling issues:\n     - NaN handling: Require relaxation stages\n     - Infeasible candidates: Use minimum-norm projection\n     - Local minima: Trigger tempering calls or dimension expansion\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Pre-check:\n     Feasibility verified by:\n        0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n        sum(h)*dx \u2248 target_sum (tolerance 1e-8)\n   - Post-optimization checks:\n     - Re-verify correlation maximum against original objective\n     - Validate output against all candidate metrics\n     - Ensure no dx scaling inconsistencies\n   - Return requirements:\n     Tuple: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n     c5_bound defined exactly as problem requires\n     h_values: normalized to [0,1] and sum=0.5\n     n_points: must match requested resolution after upsampling",
